nes[6] in the first row, but since there are only 7 lines total, the last column will be incomplete. I'm working through the fill-down algorithm for distributing lines across columns. With 7 lines and 3 columns, I calculate rows as ceil(7/3) = 3, then each column j starts at index j * rows and contains min(rows, remaining lines) entries. So for any position at row i, column j, the line index is j * rows + i, which gives me the correct mapping of 1, 4, 6 in the first row. I see the pattern now—the columns don't have uniform sizes. Column 0 contains 3 entries (lines 1-3), column 1 has 2 entries (lines 4-5), and column 2 has 2 entries (lines 6-7), so the starting indices are determined by accumulating the previous column sizes rather than a fixed stride. The key insight is that with 7 items across 3 columns, the first column gets the extra row since 7 mod 3 equals 1, leaving columns 1 and 2 with one fewer entry each.\n\nNow I need to figure out the indexing formula—for any column j and row i, I can calculate the starting position by summing up all entries in the previous columns, then add the row offset. Verifying the formula works across all positions in the grid—checking each row and column combination to ensure the index calculations map correctly to the expected values, and confirming that out-of-bounds positions correctly return empty. Writing helper functions... Completing the formatColumnsAcross function to build rows by iterating through columns and appending to the result...\n\nNow I'm implementing joinColumns to pad non-last columns to the target width, and padWithTabs to intelligently insert tabs that align to tab stops rather than using spaces.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":610,"cache_read_input_tokens":43776,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":610},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_a